1
00:00:13,950 --> 00:00:15,950
 I think that's my 28,000th plant.

2
00:00:15,950 --> 00:00:21,540
 And I think I'm close to my goal.

3
00:00:21,540 --> 00:00:26,760
 The parents, smooth yellow, wrinkled green, and the offspring.

4
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:29,760
 Yes, no mistaking it, the wrinkled green ones are recessive,

5
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:33,550
 the smooth yellow dominant.

6
00:00:33,550 --> 00:00:35,550
 I've observed for 20 years to learn that.

7
00:00:35,550 --> 00:00:39,280
 Today, children, I feel like telling you about a man who,

8
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:41,280
 you could say he had a passion for peas.

9
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:43,280
 Me too, I like him.

10
00:00:43,280 --> 00:00:44,280
 Well, good, I'm glad.

11
00:00:44,280 --> 00:00:46,280
 But I'm telling you about the father of genetics.

12
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:48,260
 I want you to solve a riddle.

13
00:00:48,260 --> 00:00:50,270
 Listen carefully, no?

14
00:00:50,270 --> 00:00:52,990
 There are six billion of them.

15
00:00:52,990 --> 00:00:56,510
 They're all other than the same, ah, but yet they're all different.

16
00:00:56,510 --> 00:00:58,880
 Reeds of sand.

17
00:00:58,880 --> 00:01:01,570
 Dan and bee, peas in a pod.

18
00:01:01,570 --> 00:01:03,230
 The leaves on the trees.

19
00:01:03,230 --> 00:01:04,990
 It's all of us.

20
00:01:04,990 --> 00:01:07,100
 Humans, yes.

21
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:09,480
 That's quite a lot, six billion.

22
00:01:09,480 --> 00:01:12,960
 One, two, three, four.

23
00:01:12,960 --> 00:01:19,330
 Can you imagine how long it would take just to count up to six billion?

24
00:01:19,330 --> 00:01:22,010
 Would be a long time. Too long.

25
00:01:22,010 --> 00:01:23,340
 Two hundred years.

26
00:01:23,340 --> 00:01:26,340
 How come they're all different and yet all the same?

27
00:01:26,340 --> 00:01:32,660
 we're all like one another because we're all human and different because no two are identical

28
00:01:32,660 --> 00:01:39,460
 these are men to women there are boys and girls some big some small blondes redheads blacks whites

29
00:01:39,460 --> 00:01:47,030
 yellows there's the strong and the sly say maestro how does it happen that we're so different and

30
00:01:47,030 --> 00:01:53,190
 why are there so many different animals yeah that's one of nature's greatest secrets and we

31
00:01:53,190 --> 00:02:01,600
 still don't comprehend it. Do you remember Darwin? Yes, your evolution. He had a long beard like, um,

32
00:02:03,070 --> 00:02:09,950
 yes, like mine. He showed that we all descended from some sort of ape. And then we all evolved,

33
00:02:09,950 --> 00:02:17,420
 evolved a lot. Have you evolved? Evolved, I'm evolved. Darwin showed that all living species

34
00:02:17,420 --> 00:02:24,510
 change over time. Yes, but how do they change? By adaptation. As I recall, Darwin observed that

35
00:02:24,510 --> 00:02:30,290
 The little finches that ate leaves had thin beaks to cut them up, and the ones that ate nuts had large beaks to break them.

36
00:02:30,370 --> 00:02:33,230
 Yes, the little finches are born different. Why is that?

37
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:34,960
 On account of the eggs.

38
00:02:34,980 --> 00:02:35,240
 Exactly.

39
00:02:35,850 --> 00:02:39,150
 Through the egg, the parent finches transmit their genes to their chicks.

40
00:02:39,420 --> 00:02:41,870
 That's pretty complicated, all that. And what's a gene?

41
00:02:42,530 --> 00:02:44,130
 I wouldn't have one.

42
00:02:44,580 --> 00:02:46,530
 Me neither. The only genes I got are blue.

43
00:02:46,610 --> 00:02:47,090
 Shut up.

44
00:02:47,090 --> 00:02:50,990
 You both have them. Birds, all animals, every living creature has genes.

45
00:02:51,640 --> 00:02:52,860
 And who discovered that?

46
00:02:53,310 --> 00:02:57,450
 A man who was a quiet genius and was interested in simple peas.

47
00:02:57,860 --> 00:03:02,560
 It all happened in Moravia, in the heart of Europe, some time ago.

48
00:03:06,540 --> 00:03:09,800
 You see, Gregor, you can graft this good branch on this apple tree.

49
00:03:10,150 --> 00:03:10,890
 How'd you do it?

50
00:03:11,250 --> 00:03:12,050
 I want to watch.

51
00:03:18,540 --> 00:03:46,030
 How is it possible for the same tree to give a different fruit?

52
00:03:46,150 --> 00:03:47,770
 That, my son, is a mystery of nature.

53
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:49,480
 You come and help me now, son.

54
00:03:53,850 --> 00:03:58,430
 Yes, sir.

55
00:03:58,670 --> 00:04:00,940
 You owe three days of work per week.

56
00:04:01,160 --> 00:04:01,810
 Come with me.

57
00:04:02,110 --> 00:04:03,910
 Yes, sir. As soon as we finish...

58
00:04:03,910 --> 00:04:06,210
 Right now! I'm not waiting. I need you.

59
00:04:15,220 --> 00:04:22,810
 Yes, sir, it's the best I've raised so far.

60
00:04:23,580 --> 00:04:33,970
 Six months.

61
00:04:35,230 --> 00:04:39,540
 But that ram, sir, it's the result of many years of selective breeding.

62
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:40,780
 I can't give him to you.

63
00:04:49,240 --> 00:05:03,270
 Did to have a ram so perfect.

64
00:05:03,650 --> 00:05:05,330
 You have to select the best parents.

65
00:05:05,610 --> 00:05:07,230
 Look, look, this used wool is excellent.

66
00:05:07,550 --> 00:05:08,950
 That ram's horns are straight.

67
00:05:09,340 --> 00:05:12,750
 They pass things on to the young ones in that way, little by little.

68
00:05:13,170 --> 00:05:14,010
 Yes, but how?

69
00:05:14,010 --> 00:05:16,550
 Ah, well, there, my lad, I really can't say.

70
00:05:17,570 --> 00:05:20,070
 Flour is fertilized by the pollen.

71
00:05:20,380 --> 00:05:21,880
 Put there, it produces a seed.

72
00:05:22,160 --> 00:05:27,820
 But how is it that the pistil and the pollen produce the seed, which then results in a flower, and not something else?

73
00:05:28,090 --> 00:05:30,550
 That, my boy, uh, we aren't sure about.

74
00:05:30,790 --> 00:05:37,450
 The lad is intelligent. He should go on with his studies, but we have so little money.

75
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:56,230
 Good man, take a look. Got a hole in his sock.

76
00:05:56,350 --> 00:05:59,130
 He's so poor he can't buy two pairs of stockings, eh?

77
00:05:59,200 --> 00:06:01,520
 A bumpkin. What's he doing here?

78
00:06:01,710 --> 00:06:02,560
 He's an idiot.

79
00:06:03,160 --> 00:06:04,660
 And you? Who, me?

80
00:06:05,010 --> 00:06:05,410
 Me?

81
00:06:05,950 --> 00:06:10,210
 Yes, you. I doubt that your fine socks make you intelligent.

82
00:06:10,720 --> 00:06:13,900
 Tell me what is required for a flower-bearing plant to produce a seed.

83
00:06:14,080 --> 00:06:15,360
 Uh, you have to shake it.

84
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:17,150
 In autumn, in autumn, in autumn, in autumn, autumn.

85
00:06:17,520 --> 00:06:19,740
 In autumn. I have to shake it in autumn.

86
00:06:20,120 --> 00:06:23,080
 You are ignorant. Now sit down.

87
00:06:23,360 --> 00:06:24,500
 What do you say, Mendel?

88
00:06:25,010 --> 00:06:28,040
 It requires that pollen penetrate into the pistol, Professor.

89
00:06:28,760 --> 00:06:30,640
 Pollen come from?

90
00:06:30,880 --> 00:06:32,640
 From the same plant or another one.

91
00:06:32,740 --> 00:06:35,100
 If it comes from a different variety, it will produce a hybrid.

92
00:06:35,530 --> 00:06:36,830
 Very good, Mendel.

93
00:06:37,080 --> 00:06:41,390
 As for you two, with your perfect silk stockings and all, Mendel could teach you a thing or two.

94
00:06:41,650 --> 00:06:42,950
 That will be all for today.

95
00:06:46,720 --> 00:06:50,790
 So Mendel could teach us that.

96
00:06:50,850 --> 00:06:53,010
 Why don't we teach him to listen, huh?

97
00:06:53,820 --> 00:06:58,630
 See that?

98
00:07:00,850 --> 00:07:02,430
 He's scared. Look at him.

99
00:07:02,720 --> 00:07:04,900
 You see, he doesn't even dare look at us.

100
00:07:05,140 --> 00:07:06,660
 It's my turn, my turn.

101
00:07:06,820 --> 00:07:07,700
 To hit him with a stone?

102
00:07:08,360 --> 00:07:10,640
 Go on, sneak back to your farm.

103
00:07:10,660 --> 00:07:11,760
 The geese are calling you.

104
00:07:11,940 --> 00:07:12,940
 Pigs are, too.

105
00:07:13,140 --> 00:07:15,300
 Look at how he's dressed. His coat's too tight.

106
00:07:15,900 --> 00:07:16,340
 Bumpkin.

107
00:07:16,420 --> 00:07:17,120
 All worn out?

108
00:07:17,510 --> 00:07:18,870
 It's true I have a hole in my sock.

109
00:07:19,090 --> 00:07:20,670
 My jacket is too small and worn.

110
00:07:21,220 --> 00:07:22,620
 You, you have fine clothes.

111
00:07:22,920 --> 00:07:26,840
 But all the same, I can teach you a little lesson.

112
00:07:29,220 --> 00:07:30,360
 Custom made my jacket.

113
00:07:30,750 --> 00:07:32,370
 It's brand new, Scottish wool.

114
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:35,200
 Well, your fine clothes are going to look like mine.

115
00:07:39,540 --> 00:07:40,260
 Jacket's ruined.

116
00:07:40,280 --> 00:07:41,660
 My socks are full of holes.

117
00:07:41,850 --> 00:07:43,930
 My shirt, just look at my shirt.

118
00:07:44,430 --> 00:07:47,230
 And if you start in again, you'll get another little lesson.

119
00:07:47,230 --> 00:07:56,020
 I'd like a piece of bread and three carrots.

120
00:07:57,450 --> 00:07:58,340
 Two carrots.

121
00:08:07,790 --> 00:08:10,640
 Come into my office, young man.

122
00:08:12,680 --> 00:08:13,190
 Here.

123
00:08:13,500 --> 00:08:15,020
 Eat this and listen to me.

124
00:08:15,720 --> 00:08:17,120
 Go on, go on, you must eat.

125
00:08:17,520 --> 00:08:19,370
 I know that you have no money.

126
00:08:19,990 --> 00:08:22,790
 And if you go on this way, Mendel, you're going to be sick.

127
00:08:23,140 --> 00:08:26,350
 But you deserve to go on with your studies, so here's what I suggest.

128
00:08:26,660 --> 00:08:29,070
 Go see the brothers at the Augustan Monastery.

129
00:08:29,900 --> 00:08:30,770
 Thank you, Professor.

130
00:08:31,270 --> 00:08:33,800
 This is the refectory where we take all our meals.

131
00:08:34,860 --> 00:08:37,020
 Here you'll be fed, don't worry.

132
00:08:37,730 --> 00:08:39,910
 Here's Brother Matthias, our botanist.

133
00:08:40,410 --> 00:08:41,910
 He takes care of our garden.

134
00:08:42,150 --> 00:08:43,130
 Come, I'll show you.

135
00:08:43,130 --> 00:08:46,480
 I'm sure that you will be very interested.

136
00:08:47,600 --> 00:08:50,620
 Tell me, are you passionately interested in botany, my son?

137
00:08:51,320 --> 00:08:55,810
 Oh, yes, Father. I'm a peasant, son of a peasant, and I've studied the natural sciences.

138
00:08:56,030 --> 00:08:59,750
 Splendid. At last I will have someone I can tell about my plants. Come and see.

139
00:09:00,070 --> 00:09:04,310
 I have many varieties of beans and peas. Come along. You know, peas are very interesting.

140
00:09:04,810 --> 00:09:06,700
 This is our common prayer chapel.

141
00:09:08,310 --> 00:09:13,510
 Generally, in nature, when plants and animals reproduce, they give birth to offspring who look like them.

142
00:09:13,810 --> 00:09:15,230
 But exceptions exist.

143
00:09:15,710 --> 00:09:16,550
 Why the exceptions?

144
00:09:17,250 --> 00:09:22,010
 Imagine these peas. These peas you see here, they're going to reproduce.

145
00:09:27,650 --> 00:09:48,050
 The rest?

146
00:09:48,050 --> 00:09:49,050
 I resemble you quite a lot.

147
00:09:49,050 --> 00:10:10,690
 Does it resemble me?

148
00:10:10,690 --> 00:10:14,590
 It's the spitting image of my dear mother.

149
00:10:14,590 --> 00:10:15,870
 Yes, you're right.

150
00:10:15,870 --> 00:10:20,500
 For humans, it's the same.

151
00:10:20,500 --> 00:10:25,800
 Why do children inherit the characteristics of their father or mother, or sometimes both?

152
00:10:25,800 --> 00:10:26,750
 Any ideas?

153
00:10:26,750 --> 00:10:28,400
 It's a mystery.

154
00:10:28,400 --> 00:10:33,400
 Why is it the peas are all yellow ones, and then for no reason, green ones suddenly reappear?

155
00:10:33,400 --> 00:10:35,400
 I've noticed other things that I can't explain.

156
00:10:35,400 --> 00:10:40,050
 I think we should carry out an experiment only.

157
00:10:40,050 --> 00:10:42,430
 It might take a long time.

158
00:10:42,430 --> 00:10:43,360
 A very long time.

159
00:10:43,360 --> 00:10:47,590
 Lunchtime.

160
00:10:47,590 --> 00:10:48,590
 We're being called to the refectory.

161
00:10:48,590 --> 00:10:51,840
 For which we give thanks, O Lord, amen.

162
00:10:57,470 --> 00:11:01,650
 A splendid sauerkraut. What a good idea. Potatoes and carrots.

163
00:11:01,650 --> 00:11:04,770
 Ah, the wonderful vegetables of Father Matthias.

164
00:11:04,770 --> 00:11:10,660
 There's nothing like it.

165
00:11:10,660 --> 00:11:11,730
 What are you planting there?

166
00:11:11,730 --> 00:11:12,690
 Peas, Father.

167
00:11:13,690 --> 00:11:14,660
 And there?

168
00:11:14,660 --> 00:11:15,610
 And there?

169
00:11:15,610 --> 00:11:17,770
 Peas, Father. And there too, Peas, Father.

170
00:11:17,770 --> 00:11:20,770
 Yeah, you're planting a great many peas, my son.

171
00:11:20,770 --> 00:11:24,790
 I'll need thousands of them for my experiment, Father.

172
00:11:24,790 --> 00:11:28,770
 Look, here all of the pea pods are green.

173
00:11:28,770 --> 00:11:30,150
 There, they're all yellow.

174
00:11:30,150 --> 00:11:33,570
 Here, the flowers are white.

175
00:11:33,570 --> 00:11:34,950
 There, the flowers are mauve.

176
00:11:34,950 --> 00:11:36,550
 Here, the plants short, they're long.

177
00:11:36,550 --> 00:11:37,990
 Oh, what energy, my son.

178
00:11:37,990 --> 00:11:39,490
 There's gonna be thousands.

179
00:11:39,490 --> 00:11:40,750
 What's the purpose, huh?

180
00:11:40,750 --> 00:11:42,920
 Well, I'll crossbreed them, Father,

181
00:11:42,920 --> 00:11:44,880
 and make hybrids and observe the results

182
00:11:44,880 --> 00:11:46,140
 over several generations.

183
00:11:46,140 --> 00:11:51,070
 And it's pea soup with little peas,

184
00:11:51,070 --> 00:11:54,040
 and little peas and a tiny slice of salted pork.

185
00:11:54,040 --> 00:11:56,670
 Let us thank the Lord for our daily bread,

186
00:11:56,670 --> 00:11:59,510
 as well as the tiny little peas that Brother Gregor grew.

187
00:11:59,510 --> 00:12:00,330
 Amen.

188
00:12:00,330 --> 00:12:02,750
 Yeah, daily bread, daily peas is all we get now.

189
00:12:02,750 --> 00:12:06,010
 You know what now, my son?

190
00:12:06,010 --> 00:12:07,710
 I go on cross-breeding peas.

191
00:12:07,710 --> 00:12:13,710
 I'll put the pollen of the pink flowers into the green pods and the white flowers into the yellow pods and the long into the short.

192
00:12:13,710 --> 00:12:15,210
 Anyway, not so fast.

193
00:12:15,210 --> 00:12:16,170
 What a lot of work.

194
00:12:16,170 --> 00:12:17,170
 Of course, I understand.

195
00:12:17,170 --> 00:12:19,170
 The fact is, my eyes aren't up to it.

196
00:12:19,170 --> 00:12:20,170
 Just a bit too weak.

197
00:12:20,170 --> 00:12:21,670
 But mine aren't much better, Father.

198
00:12:21,670 --> 00:12:23,170
 You see, I wear glasses.

199
00:12:23,170 --> 00:12:28,200
 Now I take pollen of the pink flower and put it on the pistol of the white flower.

200
00:12:28,200 --> 00:12:36,210
 Mom, Father, we're being called for a meal.

201
00:12:36,210 --> 00:12:41,300
 No, my son, leave me here. I'd like to watch the sun set one last time.

202
00:12:41,540 --> 00:12:43,790
 No, father. You're going to catch cold.

203
00:12:43,810 --> 00:12:46,910
 Don't worry about me. I have lived a very long time.

204
00:12:47,050 --> 00:12:48,830
 Remember, I will always be with you.

205
00:12:49,090 --> 00:12:51,030
 I know the work that you are doing is important.

206
00:12:51,410 --> 00:12:52,200
 Now, my son.

207
00:12:52,670 --> 00:12:53,540
 Very well, father.

208
00:12:53,600 --> 00:12:54,580
 Give me your blessing.

209
00:12:59,830 --> 00:13:05,240
 Thank you, my son. I don't want to be alone.

210
00:13:28,850 --> 00:14:00,160
 Father, my...

211
00:14:00,160 --> 00:14:15,940
 Yes.

212
00:14:22,610 --> 00:14:28,070
 It's a few potatoes, eh?

213
00:14:28,230 --> 00:14:29,130
 You plant them.

214
00:14:29,510 --> 00:14:30,280
 Very busy.

215
00:14:33,110 --> 00:14:45,630
 Start by some of your...

216
00:14:45,630 --> 00:14:48,510
 But Dana's not here, my brother.

217
00:14:48,510 --> 00:14:50,600
 You can see that we've planted peas.

218
00:14:50,600 --> 00:14:53,600
 But no more peas. We can do without peas. We don't want any more.

219
00:14:53,600 --> 00:14:55,600
 We hate them. We're gonna rip up your garden.

220
00:14:55,600 --> 00:14:59,600
 No, no. A great experiment is underway. It will revolutionize science.

221
00:14:59,600 --> 00:15:02,570
 Yeah, well, right now, there is a revolution in my stomach.

222
00:15:02,570 --> 00:15:06,630
 You can plant them. There's a good place to plant potatoes over there in the field.

223
00:15:06,630 --> 00:15:07,600
 Plenty of room.

224
00:15:07,600 --> 00:15:08,600
 But this is the cloister garden.

225
00:15:08,600 --> 00:15:12,600
 It's occupied, but you'll plant your potatoes on the other side of the wall, huh?

226
00:15:12,600 --> 00:15:19,430
 I'd like some help to get over the wall.

227
00:15:19,430 --> 00:15:21,480
 No.

228
00:15:21,480 --> 00:15:23,810
 Good.

229
00:15:23,810 --> 00:15:26,840
 Pain is right here.

230
00:15:26,860 --> 00:15:29,320
 Yeah, where's my foot?

231
00:15:30,690 --> 00:15:35,980
 That's it, I found it. Now I'm certain I'm right.

232
00:15:36,160 --> 00:15:37,560
 Brother, what did you find exactly?

233
00:15:37,640 --> 00:15:41,290
 The characteristics of plants are passed on by pairs of little particles,

234
00:15:41,450 --> 00:15:43,930
 two for color, two for shape, two for size.

235
00:15:44,190 --> 00:15:45,270
 Certain particles are dominant.

236
00:15:45,550 --> 00:15:47,980
 Look here, no question, yellow is dominant.

237
00:15:48,180 --> 00:15:49,360
 It's a dominant characteristic.

238
00:15:50,090 --> 00:15:52,670
 I'm not too sure I really understood what it meant.

239
00:15:52,990 --> 00:15:54,230
 It's pretty complicated.

240
00:15:54,610 --> 00:15:58,290
 Remember Darwin, children, in The Origin of Species, he said...

241
00:15:58,290 --> 00:16:01,270
 The laws of heredity are still largely unknown to us.

242
00:16:01,270 --> 00:16:05,290
 Back to our story. Some years later, Mendel would discover those laws of heredity.

243
00:16:05,870 --> 00:16:10,210
 What he discovered was the gene. The gene, the very basis of modern genetics.

244
00:16:11,990 --> 00:16:13,110
 I should explain a bit.

245
00:16:14,030 --> 00:16:23,090
 The pea has two genes for determining its color. One for yellow, one for green.

246
00:16:23,190 --> 00:16:27,620
 But the yellow one is dominant. If it's together with a green gene, yellow wins.

247
00:16:27,760 --> 00:16:32,680
 And the peas will have a yellow pod. But then, the next generation, what happens then?

248
00:16:32,680 --> 00:16:41,150
 You see, once again, it's the dominant yellow gene that wins over the green in here.

249
00:16:41,250 --> 00:16:42,610
 But here, what do we find?

250
00:16:42,670 --> 00:16:43,610
 Two genes are green.

251
00:16:43,730 --> 00:16:46,390
 And so the color green, which wasn't in the second generation,

252
00:16:46,550 --> 00:16:48,210
 reappears in the third generation.

253
00:16:48,820 --> 00:16:53,340
